  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Moss club (Multiclavula vernalis) is a small, delicate fungus belonging to the Hydnaceae family, characterized by its tiny club-shaped or branched fruiting bodies. Despite its small size, it plays an important ecological role in forest ecosystems by forming associations with mosses.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Miniature club shape",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are distinctly club-shaped or branched, typically 1-3 cm tall with a pale cream to whitish color",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Moss association",
      "description": "Consistently found growing directly on or among moss colonies, never on soil or wood alone",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth spore-bearing surface",
      "description": "Bears minute spines (hydnoid teeth) on its surface rather than gills or pores, visible under magnification",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Multiclavula vernalis thrives in moist, shaded forest habitats of New England where mosses are abundant, particularly on damp soil, rotting wood, or mossy logs in deciduous and mixed forests.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This fungus exhibits a fascinating symbiotic relationship with mosses, as it appears to depend on moss presence for fruiting, making it an indicator species for healthy, moisture-rich forest ecosystems.",
